"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_computer_science_en"][12]={"university_computer_science_en:M12-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M12-L1 – Imperative programming and structured data<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Programming and software quality<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning objectives<\/h2>\n<p>Design imperative data processing from a contract, formulate a loop\ninvariant, distinguish references from copies, and test ordinary and\nexceptional cases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>State, control flow and data contracts<\/h2>\n<p>An imperative program specifies changes to state through statements.\nIn Python an assignment binds a name to an object; it is not a\nmathematical assertion of equality. Sequence, branching and repetition\ncombine steps. A function contract describes accepted inputs and the\nrequired outcome. Separate this from the implementation: different\nalgorithms can satisfy the same contract.<\/p>\n<p>Model bookings as pairs <code>(name, cents)<\/code>, with a nonempty\nstring <code>name<\/code> and a nonnegative integer <code>cents<\/code>\n(excluding Boolean values). The input is a finite sequence of such\npairs. Return the sum of amounts in cents for every name encountered,\neven if its sum is zero. An invalid name or amount raises\n<code>ValueError<\/code>. Malformed pair structure is outside the\ncontract; unpacking may raise a different exception. Integer cents are\nexact in this model; floating-point values would be unnecessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Loop invariant and algorithm<\/h2>\n<p>After processing the first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npairs, <code>result<\/code> contains precisely the names encountered so\nfar and the sum of the amounts associated with each name among those\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npairs. Initialization: at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe empty dictionary satisfies the statement. Maintenance: the next\nvalid entry\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a,c)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges only the sum for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor a new name the previous sum is zero. The statement therefore holds\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTermination: at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe dictionary satisfies the contract. The finite input sequence ensures\ntermination. An invalid entry aborts without returning a partial\nresult.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> totals(rows):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    result <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> name, cents <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rows:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">isinstance<\/span>(name, <span class=\"bu\">str<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> name:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;name&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">type<\/span>(cents) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> cents <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;cents&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        result[name] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> result.get(name, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> cents<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> result<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rows <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> totals(rows) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">150<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> totals([]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> {}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> rows <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">120<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;B&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>)]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The diagram shows three state transitions. Assuming the usual average\nconstant-time dictionary operations, processing requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuch operations and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(u)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nentries for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndistinct names. This is not a worst-case guarantee for arbitrary hash\ncollisions, string lengths or arbitrarily large integers.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_061_EN.svg\" alt=\"Aggregation state sequence\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Aggregation state sequence<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Structured data and aliasing<\/h2>\n<p>A list stores an ordered sequence; tuple positions cannot be\nreassigned after construction; a dictionary maps hashable keys to\nvalues. Tuples can still contain mutable objects. Choose a structure by\nthe required operations rather than its syntax. <code>totals<\/code>\nleaves the input intact because it mutates only a new dictionary. With\naliasing, however, multiple names can refer to the same mutable\nlist.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>original <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>alias <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> original<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-3\"><a href=\"#cb2-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>shallow <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> original.copy()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-4\"><a href=\"#cb2-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>shallow[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>].append(<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-5\"><a href=\"#cb2-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> alias <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> original<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-6\"><a href=\"#cb2-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> original <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>], [<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-7\"><a href=\"#cb2-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> shallow <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> original<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>A shallow copy separates only the outer container. Deep copies can be\nuseful, but may also change intended object identities. Prefer an\nexplicit contract about ownership and permitted changes. A mutable\ndefault argument (<code>def f(xs=[]): ...<\/code>) can inadvertently\nshare state between calls; a <code>None<\/code> default and a fresh list\ncreated on each call avoid this.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Error cases and independent checking<\/h2>\n<p>A repeated name tests accumulation; empty input tests initialization;\nzero tests that an existing key is not incorrectly omitted. Negative\namounts, Boolean amounts and empty names violate the contract. Also test\nthe output against an independent specification, for example summing all\nmatching bookings separately for each name. This slower reference\nalgorithm is appropriate for small test inputs. Tests do not prove the\ninvariant in general, and the invariant does not automatically verify\nthe actual Python implementation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb3\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb3-1\"><a href=\"#cb3-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> bad <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&quot;&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&quot;A&quot;<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-2\"><a href=\"#cb3-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">try<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-3\"><a href=\"#cb3-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        totals([bad])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-4\"><a href=\"#cb3-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">except<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-5\"><a href=\"#cb3-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">pass<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-6\"><a href=\"#cb3-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-7\"><a href=\"#cb3-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">AssertionError<\/span>(bad)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>This block assumes the function from S3. Real bookkeeping would have\nadditional requirements such as currencies, refunds and transaction\nboundaries; this example performs aggregation only.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Trace <code>totals([(\"B\", 0), (\"A\", 5), (\"B\", 2)])<\/code> step by\nstep.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Formulate the invariant for a loop counting even elements.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is <code>dict.fromkeys([\"A\", \"B\"], [])<\/code> unsuitable for\nindependent lists?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give an invalid booking with correctly formed pair structure.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does space usage change if all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnames are distinct?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S6\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>{}<\/code>, <code>{\"B\": 0}<\/code>,\n<code>{\"B\": 0, \"A\": 5}<\/code>, <code>{\"B\": 2, \"A\": 5}<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">After\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\niterations the counter equals the number of even values among the first\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nelements; initially zero, maintained by conditional increment.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both keys receive the same list; a mutation is visible through both.\nUse a comprehension creating a fresh list for each key.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example <code>(\"A\", -3)<\/code>; <code>ValueError<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nentries rather than a potentially much smaller dictionary.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/tutorial/controlflow.html\">Python\n3.12 Tutorial, Control Flow<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.1–4.3, 4.8–4.9: control flow,\nfunctions, default arguments · Last accessed: 2026-11-24 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/tutorial/datastructures.html\">Python\n3.12 Tutorial, Data Structures<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §5.1, 5.3, 5.5–5.6: mutable lists,\ntuples, dictionaries · Last accessed: 2026-11-24 · Content verified:\n2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M12-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M12-L2 – Functional programming and recursion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Programming and software quality<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning objectives<\/h2>\n<p>Distinguish pure and effectful functions, combine map and fold,\ndefine recursive data, and justify termination and correctness by\nstructural induction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Pure functions and functional abstraction<\/h2>\n<p>A pure function returns the same result for the same input values and\nhas no observable side effects. Writing a file, modifying an externally\nvisible list, or querying the current time are not pure operations.\nAbsence of assignment is neither necessary nor sufficient for purity: a\nlocally mutated accumulator that does not escape can implement an\nexternally pure function. Conversely, a short expression may read hidden\nstate. Here we consider terminating functions on immutable data with\ndeterministic, pure function arguments.<\/p>\n<p>Higher-order functions take functions as arguments or return\nfunctions. <code>map(f, xs)<\/code> describes an elementwise\ntransformation and <code>filter(p, xs)<\/code> selects elements. Python\nreturns iterators for both; <code>list(...)<\/code> materializes the\nresult. A fold reduces a sequence to a single value using a binary\nfunction and an initial value. Evaluation order is part of its\ndefinition.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Folds and evaluation order<\/h2>\n<p>For a left fold,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_0=z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_{i+1}=f(a_i,x_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe empty sequence returns\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA right fold instead corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x_1,f(x_2,\\ldots,f(x_n,z)))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor subtraction with seed zero and 1,2,3 the left fold returns -6, the\nright fold 2. A different grouping is not equivalent without suitable\nalgebraic properties. For an associative operation with a two-sided\nidentity as seed, regrouping is valid; changing order additionally\nrequires commutativity (or a specific justification). Floating-point\naddition is not generally exactly associative.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> fold_left(fn, seed, xs):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    acc <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> seed<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> xs:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        acc <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> fn(acc, x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> acc<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> tree_fold(leaf, branch, tree):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">type<\/span>(tree) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> leaf(tree)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">type<\/span>(tree) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">tuple<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(tree) <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;expected int or pair&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    left, right <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> tree<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> branch(tree_fold(leaf, branch, left),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>                  tree_fold(leaf, branch, right))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>tree <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> ((<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>), <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> tree_fold(<span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x: x, <span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> a, b: a<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b, tree) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> tree_fold(<span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x: <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> a, b: a<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b, tree) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> fold_left(<span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> a, x: a<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, [<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">map<\/span>(<span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x: x<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x, [<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>])) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">16<\/span>]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The iterative left fold traverses the finite sequence with an\naccumulator; it is linear in the number of elements if the function\nargument takes constant time. A Python iterator can be single-use. The\ndiagram illustrates folding a binary tree.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_062_EN.svg\" alt=\"Tree fold from leaves to root\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Tree fold from leaves to\nroot<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Recursive data types and induction proof<\/h2>\n<p>A finite full binary tree in this model is either an integer leaf or\na pair of such trees. Cycles are excluded. <code>tree_fold<\/code>\nhandles leaves with <code>leaf<\/code> and internal nodes with\n<code>branch<\/code>. Termination follows from the number of nodes as a\nnatural-number measure: both recursive arguments are proper subtrees\nwith fewer nodes. Correctness of leaf summation follows by structural\ninduction. Leaf: <code>leaf(x)=x<\/code> gives the sum. Pair: if both\nrecursive results are the respective leaf sums, adding them gives the\nsum of all leaves. Similarly <code>leaf(x)=1<\/code> counts leaves. The\nfunction arguments can be chosen independently of the traversal\nscheme.<\/p>\n<p>For <code>((2,3),4)<\/code> the partial sums are 5 and 4, then 9. A\nfull binary tree with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nleaves has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninternal nodes (induction on the same structure); assuming constant node\ncosts the traversal takes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntime and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O(h+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncall stack for height\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin edges. A highly skewed tree may hit Python’s recursion limit even\nthough the mathematical recursion terminates. Iterative postorder\ntraversal using an explicit stack is an alternative.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Practice: separating computation from effects<\/h2>\n<p>A functional core can derive a new result value from an immutable\ninput model. An outer imperative layer reads input, invokes the core and\nwrites results. This simplifies repeatable tests but does not guarantee\ncorrectness. For example, a function argument can have a hidden effect.\nThe contract of <code>tree_fold<\/code> requires a valid finite tree and\nterminating leaf and branch functions; for the mathematical\ninterpretation as a pure fold these must also be pure. Invalid non-pairs\nare rejected, and excessively deep trees may raise\n<code>RecursionError<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>A test generator can enumerate all small tree shapes and collect\ntheir leaves with an explicit stack traversal. Compare leaf sum and\ncount against the fold. Use different node operations to detect\naccidental argument reversal, such as concatenating tuples in\nleft-to-right order.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the left fold with multiplication and seed 1 for\n2,3,4.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the right fold with subtraction and seed 0 for 1,2,3.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give leaf sum and count for <code>(1,(2,(3,4)))<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is reading the current time inside a function not pure?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Formulate a tree fold for maximum leaf depth in edges.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S6\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">24.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-(2-(3-0))=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Sum 10, count 4.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The same explicit input can yield different results; the clock is an\nexternal source of state.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>tree_fold(lambda x: 0, lambda a,b: 1+max(a,b), tree)<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.composingprograms.com/pages/16-higher-order-functions.html\">Composing\nPrograms, Higher-Order Functions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §1.6.1, 1.6.8: functional\nabstraction · Last accessed: 2026-11-24 · Content verified:\n2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.composingprograms.com/pages/17-recursive-functions.html\">Composing\nPrograms, Recursive Functions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §1.7.1, 1.7.4: base cases, recursive\ndecomposition · Last accessed: 2026-11-24 · Content verified:\n2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M12-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M12-L3 – Object orientation, types and polymorphism<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Programming and software quality<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning objectives<\/h2>\n<p>Explain identity, state and interfaces; implement structural\npolymorphism and assess whether a subtype satisfies a behavioral\ncontract. Choose between inheritance and composition with reasons.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Objects and type contracts<\/h2>\n<p>A class defines a type and supplies methods; an object is a\nparticular instance with identity and potentially mutable state. Two\nnames can refer to the same object: modifying a mutable list through one\nalias is visible through the other. Value equality differs from identity\n(<code>==<\/code> versus <code>is<\/code>). Encapsulation directs\npermitted state changes through a defined interface. In Python a leading\nunderscore is a convention, not a security boundary. A frozen dataclass\nprevents ordinary attribute assignment but does not guarantee deep\nimmutability of nested objects.<\/p>\n<p>A type contract describes accepted inputs, results and observable\neffects. Nominal subtyping relies on declared type relationships;\nstructural subtyping relies on the required interface. Python supports\ndynamic method calls; <code>Protocol<\/code> expresses structural typing\nrequirements for static analysis. Type annotations generally do not\nenforce input validation at runtime.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Composition and polymorphism<\/h2>\n<p>We model pricing in integer accounting units without rounding. The\n<code>Charge.cost<\/code> contract accepts a nonnegative integer\nquantity, returns a nonnegative integer price, and does not change the\ntariff object. The linear tariff charges 3 per unit; the tiered tariff\ncharges 3 for each of the first ten units and 2 for each additional\nunit. Both implementations raise <code>ValueError<\/code> for invalid\nquantities. We deliberately reject <code>bool<\/code>, although Python\nderives it from <code>int<\/code>. The <code>Quote<\/code> class delegates\nto a tariff without inspecting its concrete type.<\/p>\n<p>The implementation demonstrates composition and dynamic method\nselection. Both tariffs satisfy the structural interface without\nexplicitly inheriting from <code>Charge<\/code>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> dataclasses <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> dataclass<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> typing <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Protocol<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">class<\/span> Charge(Protocol):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> cost(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>, units: <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">-&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>: ...<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> check_units(units: <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">-&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">type<\/span>(units) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> units <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;nonnegative integer required&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"at\">@dataclass<\/span>(frozen<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">class<\/span> Linear:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> cost(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>, units: <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">-&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        check_units(units)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> units<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"at\">@dataclass<\/span>(frozen<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">class<\/span> Tiered:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> cost(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>, units: <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">-&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        check_units(units)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(units, <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, units <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"at\">@dataclass<\/span>(frozen<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">class<\/span> Quote:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    tariff: Charge<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> total(<span class=\"va\">self<\/span>, units: <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">-&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"va\">self<\/span>.tariff.cost(units)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> Quote(Linear()).total(<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">36<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> Quote(Tiered()).total(<span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">34<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_063_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Quote delegates through the interface to two implementations\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Quote delegates through the interface to\ntwo implementations<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Substitution and invariants<\/h2>\n<p>The substitution principle requires a concrete type to work wherever\nits contract is expected. A subtype must not strengthen required\npreconditions, weaken promised postconditions or violate promised\ninvariants. A class accepting at most ten units does not satisfy this\ncontract despite an identical method signature. Returning -1 for a valid\ninput also violates the contract. A static type checker can detect\nsignature errors but cannot generally prove all semantic promises.<\/p>\n<p>For <code>Tiered<\/code>, the price is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\leq n\\leq10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30+2(n-10)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&gt;10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe expressions agree at the boundary and every extra unit increases the\nprice. The contract does not require a particular rate, however. The\nprice formula is a stronger property of the individual tariff, not the\nshared protocol.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Design decisions<\/h2>\n<p>Inheritance is suitable for a stable is-a relationship with a\nmatching contract, but it may couple subclasses to base-class internals.\nComposition expresses a has-a relationship: <code>Quote<\/code> contains\na tariff, and a new <code>Quote<\/code> can be constructed with a\ndifferent tariff. This example uses immutable, stateless tariffs, not\nshared mutable prices. A discount decorator could contain another tariff\nprovided it preserves the contract, including rounding rules. Real\nfinancial calculations would require separate currency, rounding and\nvalidity-period specifications.<\/p>\n<p>Check every tariff with the same contract tests (0, 1, 10, 11,\nnegative input). Also check the particular price independently.\nSignatures, contract tests and domain specifications complement one\nanother.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distinguish identity and value equality.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate both tariffs for 10 and 13 units.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a tariff limited to ten units violate the common\ncontract?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does <code>Protocol<\/code> not enforce runtime validation\nhere?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Design a constant surcharge of 5 using composition. What property is\npreserved?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S6\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Identity concerns the same object; equality concerns the defined\nvalue-equality relation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">10: both 30; 13: linear 39, tiered 36.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It strengthens the precondition; the interface accepts every\nnonnegative integer quantity.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Annotations support static analysis; <code>check_units<\/code>\nperforms explicit runtime validation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A wrapper holds a <code>Charge<\/code> and returns\n<code>cost(units)+5<\/code> after calling it. Nonnegative integer results\nand error behavior remain intact if the wrapped tariff meets the\ncontract.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/tutorial/classes.html\">Python\n3.12, Classes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · 9.1, 9.3, 9.5: objects, methods, inheritance · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-24 · Content verified: 2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3.12/library/typing.html\">Python\n3.12, typing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · type annotations; Protocol and structural subtyping ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-24 · Content verified: 2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_computer_science_en:M12-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M12-L4 – Test design and systematic software quality<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Programming and software quality<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor’s · Core/foundations<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L1<\/a>; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_computer-science&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning objectives<\/h2>\n<p>Derive tests from a specification, select equivalence classes and\nboundaries, formulate properties, and evaluate coverage and mutation\ntesting.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Specification and test oracle<\/h2>\n<p>A test needs an observable expected result or another test oracle. A\nunit test examines a small unit in isolation; an integration test\nexamines cooperation among units. System tests assess the complete\nsystem against requirements. Regression tests protect against recurrence\nof known defects. These categories may overlap. Test inputs and expected\nresults should not merely duplicate the implementation logic, since\nshared mistakes may remain undetected.<\/p>\n<p>Our specification classifies integer scores from 0 to 100: 0–49 yield\n<code>basic<\/code>, 50–79 yield <code>standard<\/code>, and 80–100 yield\n<code>advanced<\/code>. Other inputs, including Boolean and\nfloating-point values, are invalid. The three valid intervals and\ninvalid input classes guide test selection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equivalence classes and boundaries<\/h2>\n<p>Equivalence classes group inputs expected to behave similarly under\nthe specification. One representative cannot prove the entire class. For\ndiscrete intervals, boundaries and their immediate neighbors are useful:\n-1, 0, 1, 48, 49, 50, 51, 78, 79, 80, 81, 99, 100, 101. Add type errors\nsuch as <code>True<\/code> and <code>50.0<\/code>. The diagram marks the\ncentral transitions.<\/p>\n<p>The implementation and small test suite are deliberately separate.\nTests expect fixed specified results and explicitly handle expected\nexceptions.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> band(x: <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">-&gt;<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">str<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">type<\/span>(x) <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> x <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;integer in 0..100 required&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;basic&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;standard&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;advanced&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> check_cases(fn):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    cases <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;basic&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">49<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;basic&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;standard&quot;<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>             (<span class=\"dv\">79<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;standard&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;advanced&quot;<\/span>), (<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;advanced&quot;<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x, expected <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> cases:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> fn(x) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> expected<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">101<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">50.0<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">try<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            fn(x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">except<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">ValueError<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">continue<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">raise<\/span> <span class=\"pp\">AssertionError<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&quot;invalid input accepted&quot;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>check_cases(band)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rank <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&quot;basic&quot;<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;standard&quot;<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"st\">&quot;advanced&quot;<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(rank[band(x)] <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> rank[band(x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-computer-science-en/media/img/U_COS_064_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three score intervals with boundary values 49, 50, 79 and 80\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three score intervals with boundary\nvalues 49, 50, 79 and 80<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Properties and coverage<\/h2>\n<p>Property-based tests check statements over many inputs. For valid\nscores, category rank must not decrease as the score rises. The code\nchecks all 100 adjacent valid pairs, establishing monotonicity for this\nfinite range. Monotonicity alone is insufficient: a function returning\n<code>basic<\/code> for every valid score also satisfies it. Boundary\ntests refute that incorrect function. Since the valid domain is small,\nall 101 values can be checked against an independently constructed\ntable. Unbounded inputs or complex state spaces still require selective\ntesting.<\/p>\n<p>Statement coverage indicates which statements executed. Branch\ncoverage checks both outcomes of a decision. Neither alone says whether\nan oracle verifies the correct result. A test that merely calls a\nfunction may miss defects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mutation testing and release<\/h2>\n<p>Mutation testing deliberately changes a program, for example\nreplacing <code>x &lt; 50<\/code> with <code>x &lt;= 50<\/code>. A mutant\nis killed when at least one test distinguishes it from the\nspecification. Here, 50 is the counterexample. Changing\n<code>x &lt; 80<\/code> to <code>x &lt; 79<\/code> is detected at 79.\nRaising the upper accepted input boundary to 101 is detected by the test\nat 101. An equivalent mutant such as replacing <code>x &lt; 50<\/code>\nwith <code>x &lt;= 49<\/code> for already validated integers cannot be\ndistinguished behaviorally.<\/p>\n<p>A mutation score of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k/m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrefers to the selected set of non-equivalent mutants; unknown\nequivalence complicates interpretation. A high score does not prove\nabsence of defects. Supplement testing with review, static analysis and\nintegration tests. A CI pipeline runs small tests on changes; failures\nblock release until the cause is resolved or the specification is\ncorrected with justification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name the three valid equivalence classes.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which values matter at the transition to <code>advanced<\/code>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which test kills the mutation <code>x &lt; 50<\/code> to\n<code>x &lt;= 50<\/code>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is monotonicity insufficient as the sole property?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Of five mutants, four are non-equivalent and three of those are\nkilled. Calculate the mutation score.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S6\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">0–49, 50–79, 80–100.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">79 and 80; additionally 78 and 81.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><code>band(50)<\/code> must yield <code>standard<\/code>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The constant output <code>basic<\/code> is also monotonic but\nincorrect.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">5.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">3/4 = 75%. The equivalent mutant is excluded from the\ndenominator.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.fuzzingbook.org/html/Intro_Testing.html\">The\nFuzzing Book, Introduction to Software Testing<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Simple Testing;\nGenerating Tests; Limits of Testing · Last accessed: 2026-11-24 ·\nContent verified: 2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.fuzzingbook.org/html/MutationAnalysis.html\">The\nFuzzing Book, Mutation Analysis<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Why Structural Coverage is Not\nEnough; Evaluating Mutations; Equivalent Mutants · Last accessed:\n2026-11-24 · Content verified: 2026-11-24<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
